  "case_id": "01_hana_indirect_memory",
  "composer": {
    "accepted_prose": "Hana set the last plate into the drying rack and wiped her hands on a kitchen towel, her movements unhurried even as Ted's question lingered in the warm, dinner-scented air. She looked at him across the table, her gaze soft but not evasive.\n\n\"You're asking about the years when the calls didn't come,\" she said, her voice warm and steady. \"I remember them. Nights when I'd watch the phone, thinking perhaps tonight, and then another night would pass. But you see, I sent him away with care, Ted. I expected the separation to be short, and when it wasn't, I chose to keep believing in him. That choice became a kind of quiet place I returned to.\"\n\nShe paused, her fingers absently straightening the edge of the tablecloth. \"There were moments I felt uneasy—brief ones. And then I would correct myself, search for the benign reason. Because trust felt more true to who I am. More faithful to what I promised him.\"\n\nA small, self-conscious smile touched her lips. \"I don't know if that answers your question. But you waited for my answer, so I'm giving you the honest one.\"",
